		Distribution notice.
When distributing a program which makes use of AftGangAglay, the following
files' contents must be accessible by the user of the program directly within
the runtime.

`vendor/libtiff/COPYRIGHT'
`vendor/python/LICENCE'
`vendor/www/Copyright.txt' and the stated required contents from
`https://www.w3.org/copyright/software-license/'
as well as the root copyright notice of AftGangAglay itself as found in the
root `LICENCE' file.

		GLM.
GLM has an unknown licence status. Attempts have been made to determine the
origin -- i.e. whether it was written by Nate Robins while interning at SGI --
and terms of use of the program; including attempts to contact both Robins
himself, the Scientific Computing and Imaging Institute at the University of
Utah and associated developers (Mark J. Kilgard). We have made the tentative
assumption that its use is permissible given the context of its broad
distribution and use in demos. In the event further information is discovered,
a licence will be placed in amendment to the archive repo, or its use will be
discontinued if the terms prove incompatible.

		libTIFF.
libTIFF uses a variant of the MIT licence with an advertising clause, which
is compatible with the terms of the MIT/X Consortium licence used by
AftGangAglay.

		Python.
Python uses a bespoke licence, which is compatible with the terms of the
MIT/X Consortium licence used by AftGangAglay.

		libWWW.
libWWW as-present within the repository does not contain the complete terms of
use as were present during its window of distribution -- as the copyright
notice defers "[t]he conditions for public domain and other access to the code"
to "[the] distribution conditions of WWW code". However, in 1994 libWWW was
re-licenced by the newly-established W3C under a permissive Open Source licence
now referred to as the "W3C Software Notice and License" -- whose latest
version can be found here: https://www.w3.org/copyright/software-license/. The
library's use is assumed to fall under this relicence due to the nature of the
external reference in the copyright notice.

The "W3C Software Notice and License" is compatible with the terms of the
MIT/X Consortium licence used by AftGangAglay.
